const pikoArticleFocus={
 'qc-photo-review':{heading:'QC photos are a decision point, not a product gallery',opening:'This guide explains how to use Pikobuy’s published warehouse-inspection flow to decide whether an item is ready to keep, return or clarify before a parcel is submitted.',focus:'Start with the exact order: colour, size, quantity and seller option. Then compare the inspection photos with that record. Ask only questions that can change your next action: whether the received variation matches, whether a visible defect exists, whether a measurement matters, and whether all expected pieces are present.',decision:'A good QC review ends with one of three decisions: keep the item, request clarification or start a return request while it is still eligible. It does not promise authenticity, material composition or hidden construction that cannot be established from the available evidence.'},
 'warehouse-storage':{heading:'Warehouse storage is a working stage, not a pause button',opening:'Pikobuy’s public forwarding terms say that goods are photographed for confirmation and then stored after warehouse receipt. This guide turns that simple stage into a useful decision workflow.',focus:'Create a short warehouse record for every received item: order number, seller link, selected option, arrival date, photo review result and return deadline where applicable. Storage becomes safer when every item has a clear status instead of an assumed “ready to ship” label.',decision:'Do not submit a combined parcel merely because several items are visible in the warehouse. Submit only after each item has passed its own review, any return choice has been resolved and the selected route accepts the parcel’s product type and destination.'},
 'actual-vs-volumetric-weight':{heading:'Estimate freight with both weight and packed dimensions in view',opening:'Pikobuy’s shipping-estimate page asks for a destination, product type, weight, length, width and height. Its beginner guide also says routes differ in delivery time and billing method. This is why a product price alone cannot predict the final international freight charge.',focus:'Treat the first shipping estimate as a planning tool. The parcel can change after repacking, protective material, shoe boxes, consolidation and route selection. Heavy compact goods and light bulky goods can produce very different outcomes, so record both the physical-weight estimate and the expected package dimensions.',decision:'The decision to keep or remove a bulky item should be made before parcel submission, not after a payment page reveals a higher quote. Use the route and parcel checkout as the current source of the payable amount; do not rely on a social-media rate, an old calculator result or another buyer’s parcel.'},
 'tracking-status':{heading:'Read tracking as a sequence of hand-offs, not a countdown',opening:'Pikobuy’s beginner guide says tracking information will be available within three days after a parcel is shipped, while also warning that international shipping takes longer. This guide helps turn status messages into a useful record rather than a reason to guess.',focus:'Keep the parcel number, selected route, payment date, dispatch status and each carrier event in one note. An event that appears later than expected can reflect a hand-off or a carrier scan timing issue; it does not by itself identify the cause or guarantee the next delivery date.',decision:'If you need support, ask one narrow question using the parcel number and the latest dated event. Avoid describing a parcel as lost, customs-cleared or delayed beyond recovery before the carrier record and the route’s current service information support that conclusion.'},
 'customs-declaration':{heading:'A declaration is destination compliance, not a price-setting tool',opening:'Pikobuy’s shipping terms state that cross-border logistics risks include customs policies and other uncontrollable factors. The platform can offer shipping services, but destination rules still apply to the importer and the parcel.',focus:'Use accurate, lawful item information and check the import requirements for the destination before submitting a parcel. Do not treat a shipping line name, a past shipment or a generic community tip as proof that a specific product is permitted, tax-free or exempt from documentation.',decision:'Where a route, product type or declaration field is unclear, pause before payment and review the current parcel form and destination guidance. A correct record is more useful than an artificial description designed to chase a particular outcome.'},
 'after-sales-evidence':{heading:'Useful after-sales evidence is specific, dated and connected',opening:'Pikobuy’s return terms make the warehouse stage important: eligible returns require seller support, resale condition and a request within the stated warehouse window. Evidence is most useful when it connects the ordered option, warehouse photos and the requested resolution.',focus:'Save the seller listing, chosen variation, order status, inspection images, relevant close-up request, arrival or in-warehouse time, and any parcel photos after dispatch. State what differs and where it is visible. A vague “looks wrong” message is harder to review than a concise, dated comparison.',decision:'A return result can depend on seller consent, product eligibility and condition. Preserve the evidence first, then make a clear request while the relevant window is open. Do not assume an item qualifies merely because it is in the warehouse or because another category was once returnable.'}
};
function pikoLongArticle(slug){const s=pikoArticleFocus[slug];return `<section class="section"><span class="eyebrow">Pikobuy platform research · reviewed 18 September 2026</span><h2>${s.heading}</h2><p>${s.opening}</p><h2>What the published flow actually says</h2><p>Pikobuy’s public beginner guide describes a six-stage path: select an item, search by product link or keywords, select specifications and make the first payment, receive warehouse inspection, choose a shipping route and submit the parcel, then wait for tracking. The same guide says the warehouse process includes check-in, photo confirmation and a defect check. It also says users may add packing requests such as minimal or reinforced packaging. These points establish a useful order of operations, but they are not a quality guarantee, an authenticity service or a delivery-time promise.</p><p>The public home page separately says that items undergo quality inspection and repacking when they arrive at the China warehouse. That is helpful operational information, yet it should not be overstated. A photo review can show the item that reached the warehouse and any visible issue in the captured view. It cannot reliably prove every measurement, internal component, material claim or future condition in transit. Good buyers use the available evidence to make a bounded decision rather than turning a warehouse image into certainty.</p><h2>QC first, then decide whether the return window matters</h2><p>Pikobuy’s returns page calls the service a “5-Day Return Guarantee” and explains the timing precisely: an application may be made within five days after the order changes to In Warehouse, counted from the next hour after the status changes; five days equal 120 hours. Its terms also say that an item is returnable only when the seller offers a return guarantee, the item remains in the seller-required resalable condition and it has been in the warehouse no more than five days. That is a conditional route, not a blanket right for every product.</p><p>Before acting, check the category and condition. The published exclusions include, among others, customised items, second-hand goods, some intimate apparel, food and items with removed packaging, labels or accessories. Some product groups have additional resale conditions; for example, sealed packaging may need to remain sealed, and a new sneaker’s anti-theft buckle or tamper-proof sticker must not be removed where applicable. The practical lesson is simple: do not request a destructive inspection or remove a seal before understanding how that choice could affect a return.</p><p>${s.focus}</p><p>${s.decision}</p><h2>Storage and parcel submission: keep the two decisions separate</h2><p>The forwarding terms say goods are photographed for confirmation when received and then stored in the warehouse. The public pages reviewed for this article describe that storage step but do not state a universal free-storage duration. For that reason, do not publish or rely on an invented number of “free days.” Check the current account, order page or parcel form for any applicable storage rule at the time of the order. A warehouse status is a prompt to review, not evidence that every item is automatically ready for consolidation.</p><p>When the item is approved, parcel submission is the next distinct transaction. Pikobuy’s guide says the buyer chooses a suitable shipping route, submits the parcel and pays international shipping; the forwarding terms say to locate the forwarded goods in the order page, submit the parcel and pay the shipping fee. Keeping product purchase and international shipping separate helps with planning. The first payment is not a final landed-cost quote, and the later shipping payment is not a guarantee against route, carrier or customs risk.</p><h2>International freight: use the calculator as an estimate, not a promise</h2><p>The current Pikobuy shipping-estimate page requests destination, product type, weight and length, width and height. The beginner guide says that routes differ in delivery time and billing methods. These public inputs are why a careful estimate includes both physical weight and packed size. They do not support a universal formula or a universal cheapest route. Packing, consolidation, route selection, restrictions and the carrier’s applicable method can all affect the quote shown for a particular parcel.</p><p>For practical planning, write down a conservative parcel scenario before you submit: destination, product type, expected weight, expected dimensions, whether original boxes are kept, desired protection and the maximum shipping amount you are willing to accept. Then compare that plan with the actual route options presented for your parcel. If the price exceeds the plan, the useful decision is to edit the parcel while it is still editable—not to assume that a later adjustment will undo the cost.</p><h2>Tracking, customs and risk language</h2><p>After dispatch, Pikobuy says tracking information will be available within three days, and its guide notes that international shipping takes longer. Record the date rather than interpreting the statement as a delivery guarantee. The shipping terms also say parcels are carried by third-party logistics providers and that customs policies, confiscation, damage, loss and peak-season delay are among cross-border risks that cannot be fully controlled. A responsible guide should report that plainly instead of promising a route will be safe, tax-free or on time.</p><p>For customs, use accurate and lawful information for the destination. The platform’s public risk notice is not destination-specific legal advice. Requirements may depend on the country, commodity, value, recipient and current rules. Check the information offered in the live parcel flow and the relevant official destination requirements before submitting. Do not use a made-up description or value to pursue an outcome; that can create a larger problem than a conservative shipping estimate.</p><h2>A checklist that makes support questions answerable</h2><p>Keep one record per item and one record per parcel. For an item, save the product link, seller, selected variant, quantity, price paid, order status, in-warehouse time, inspection images and any photo request. For a parcel, save the chosen items, route, estimated and final dimensions/weight where shown, shipping payment, tracking number and dated events. This is not busywork: it stops a product question from becoming confused with a parcel question.</p><p>If a problem appears, state the evidence and the requested action. For a QC issue, identify the selected option and the visible difference. For a possible return, verify the seller’s eligibility, the in-warehouse time and the item’s condition before requesting it. For a tracking question, include the parcel number and latest dated event. Pikobuy’s returns page notes that the total refund is based on the actual product price paid and that a return can only be made with seller consent. A precise request is more useful than an accusation or a promise the published terms do not make.</p><h2>Bottom line</h2><p>Use the platform’s sequence in order: select accurately, pay the product stage, review warehouse evidence promptly, make the return decision within any applicable window, store only after each item is resolved, then submit a parcel and accept the current route quote. Treat QC, storage, international freight, tracking and customs as related but separate decisions. This approach is slower than guessing from a listing photo, but it is the clearest way to protect the facts that will matter when you decide to keep, return, consolidate or ask a focused support question.</p></section>`}
function seoArticle(l,slug){const t=langs[l],x=labels[l],a=articles[l].find(v=>v[0]===slug);return page(l,a[1],`<p class="crumb"><a href="/${l}/">${t.home}</a> / <a href="/${l}/seo-articles/">${x.articles}</a> / ${a[1]}</p><section class="section"><span class="eyebrow">${x.articles}</span><h1>${a[1]}</h1><p>${a[2]}</p></section>${pikoLongArticle(slug)}<section class="section"><a class="button" href="https://findspreadsheet.com/AllProducts/">${t.db}</a></section>`,a[2])}
function category(l,c){const t=langs[l],items=products[c[0]];return page(l,`${c[1]} selection guide`, `<p class="crumb"><a href="/${l}/">${t.home}</a> / <a href="/${l}/categories/">${t.cats}</a> / ${c[1]}</p><section class="hero"><div><span class="eyebrow">${t.logic}</span><h1>${c[1]}: choose with evidence</h1><p>${c[3]}</p><a class="button" href="https://findspreadsheet.com${c[2]}">${t.db}</a></div><div class="product-rail category-products" aria-label="${c[1]} products">${items.map(p=>productTile(p,t)).join('')}</div></section><section class="section split"><div><span class="eyebrow">${t.check}</span><h2>${c[4]}</h2><p>Use the seller listing and warehouse evidence to compare the exact chosen variation. Save photos and measurements before international shipping.</p></div><aside class="case"><span class="pill">${t.case}</span><h3>${c[1]}</h3><p>${c[5]}</p></aside></section><section class="section"><h2>Next useful step</h2><p>When the category direction is clear, open the live FindSpreadsheet category. For order-process questions, use the buying and QC guides first.</p><a class="button" href="/${l}/guides/qc-workflow/">${t.qc}</a></section>`,c[3])}
function guide(l,kind){const t=langs[l];const copy={
 'how-to-order':['A practical Joyagoo buying flow','Start with a working product link or search term. Select size, colour and quantity before payment. Public guidance says product payment includes the item and China domestic shipping; international shipping happens later when the parcel is submitted. Warehouse QC photos are a decision point before shipping.'],
 'qc-workflow':['QC workflow: make photos answer a decision','QC photos are useful only when they answer an agreed question: is this the selected variation, are the dimensions acceptable, is there visible damage, and is the set complete? Joyagoo says it performs a QC process after warehouse arrival and takes several product photos. Review them promptly because returns have a time limit.'],
 'shipping-cost':['Shipping cost: plan after the item is confirmed','Joyagoo explains that the final international fee can depend on the packed parcel’s actual size and weight, not only a product’s listed price. Keep bulky items, restricted categories and optional packing services visible in your plan.'],
 'country-support':['Countries and destination research','Joyagoo describes itself as a global shopping and shipping agent. Its public shipping page names USA Line, UK Line, Europe DHL Line, Europe Tariffless Line and European Railway among its logistics arrangements. That supports creating US, UK and Europe information paths, but it does not prove a route is available for every product, address or date.']};const [h,p]=copy[kind];return page(l,h,`<p class="crumb"><a href="/${l}/">${t.home}</a> / ${h}</p><section class="section"><span class="eyebrow">Platform facts + independent advice</span><h1>${h}</h1><p>${p}</p></section><section class="section grid"><article class="card"><h3>Platform fact</h3><p>Use the official service for account actions, payment, order status, warehouse work, parcel submission and shipping choices.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Independent action</h3><p>Use this site to document your chosen variation, compare category-specific QC checks, then open the product database when you are ready.</p></article></section><section class="section"><h2>${t.countries}</h2><p>${t.countryText}</p><a class="button" href="https://findspreadsheet.com/AllProducts/">${t.db}</a></section>`,p)}
const pikoFaq=[
 ['Is this the official PIKOBUY website?','No. This is an independent research guide for product discovery. It does not create PIKOBUY accounts, take payment, approve QC, submit parcels or provide shipping service.'],
 ['What does PIKOBUY’s public buying flow say?','Its beginner guide describes selecting an item, searching by a product link or keywords, choosing specifications and paying for the order, warehouse inspection, parcel submission and international-shipping payment, then tracking after shipment.'],
 ['What happens when an item reaches the warehouse?','PIKOBUY says the warehouse process includes check-in, photo confirmation and a defect check. Its home page also says items are inspected and repacked on arrival. Review the evidence as soon as the status changes instead of treating a warehouse arrival as automatic approval.'],
 ['Do QC photos guarantee that an item is perfect or authentic?','No. Photos can help confirm the received variation and visible condition in the captured view. They cannot prove every hidden detail, material claim, measurement or future condition during international transit.'],
 ['Is there usually a five-day return window after QC?','PIKOBUY’s public returns page calls this a 5-Day Return Guarantee. It says requests can be made within five days after the status becomes In Warehouse, counted from the next hour; five days equal 120 hours. Eligibility still depends on the seller, item condition and product category.'],
 ['Which items qualify for the five-day return service?','The published terms say the seller must offer the guarantee, the item must meet the seller’s resale condition and it must have been in the warehouse no more than five days. This is not a universal return right.'],
 ['Which products may be excluded from returns?','The published examples include customised goods, second-hand products, some intimate apparel, food and products whose packaging, labels or accessories were removed at the user’s request. Some categories also have special sealing or condition requirements.'],
 ['Who pays when returning an item?','For an unconditional return, the published fee is return shipping to the seller, the seller’s original shipping fee and RMB 5 service fee. The terms say responsibility can differ when the seller shipped the wrong item or a verified quality issue exists. Confirm the current order page before acting.'],
 ['Does PIKOBUY publish one universal warehouse-storage period?','The public pages reviewed for this guide say received goods are photographed and stored, but do not state a universal free-storage length. Check the live account, order page or parcel form for the rule applying to your order.'],
 ['When is international shipping paid?','The beginner guide says the buyer chooses a shipping route, submits the parcel and pays the international shipping fee after the warehouse stage. The first product payment is therefore not the final international freight payment.'],
 ['Why should I consider both actual weight and dimensions?','PIKOBUY’s shipping-estimate page asks for weight plus length, width and height, while its guide says billing methods differ by route. Use both physical weight and packed size for planning; the route shown at parcel submission is the current source for the payable quote.'],
 ['Can I rely on another buyer’s shipping price?','No. Destination, product type, packed dimensions, physical weight, packing choices and the available route can change a quote. Use a past price only as a rough comparison, not as a commitment.'],
 ['When should tracking appear?','PIKOBUY’s beginner guide says tracking information will be available within three days after shipment and notes that international shipping takes longer. That is a tracking-availability statement, not a delivery-date promise.'],
 ['What does PIKOBUY say about customs and transit risk?','Its shipping terms say parcels are carried by third-party logistics providers and list customs policies, confiscation, damage, loss and peak-season delays among cross-border risks. Check destination rules and use accurate, lawful declaration information.'],
 ['What evidence should I save for an after-sales question?','Keep the seller listing, exact selected option, order status, in-warehouse time, inspection photos, relevant photo requests, parcel number and dated tracking events. A specific record supports a clearer question than a generic complaint.']
];
function faq(l){const t=langs[l];return page(l,'Pikobuy buying questions',`<section class="section faq"><span class="eyebrow">${t.faq} · Pikobuy research</span><h1>Practical PIKOBUY questions, answered from public terms</h1><p class="muted">Reviewed 18 September 2026. Platform policies, routes, fees and destination requirements can change; check the live order or parcel page before paying.</p>${pikoFaq.map((q,i)=>`<details ${i===0?'open':''}><summary>${q[0]}</summary><p>${q[1]}</p></details>`).join('')}</section>`,t.intro)}
